      ["Definition / range", "Use **about 37.5-38.0 C / 99.5-100.4 F** as a practical NCLEX study anchor for low-grade fever. Some references define fever at **>=38.0 C / 100.4 F**, so always compare with facility policy, temperature route, baseline, and the client's condition."],
      ["Why nurses care", "Low-grade fever can be an early cue for infection, inflammation, transfusion reaction, medication reaction, post-op complication, autoimmune flare, dehydration, heat illness, or normal variation depending on the situation. Trend it with heart rate, respiratory rate, blood pressure, pain, mental status, WBC/ANC, cultures, wounds, lines, and symptoms."],
      ["High-risk clients", "Escalate sooner for **neutropenia**, immunosuppression, chemotherapy, newborns or young infants, older adults with new confusion or weakness, pregnancy/postpartum infection symptoms, central venous catheters, fresh surgical wounds, burns, and suspected sepsis."],
      ["NCLEX trap", "Do not overreact to one mild number by itself, but do not dismiss a low-grade fever when the clinical story is unsafe. The exam cares about **trend + risk + source + perfusion**, not temperature alone."]
